      "itemDescription": "The U.S.\u2019s largest health care facility operator reported a decrease in profit and revenue growth in 2022 versus the year prior, partly due to the phasing out of government assistance that accompanied the pandemic.<br />\r\n<ul><br />\r\n \t<li>HCA Healthcare, which operates in 20 states in the U.S., has in aggregate removed hospitals from its portfolio and added outpatient surgery centers since 2020, reflecting a broader shift to outpatient care among hospital conglomerates.</li><br />\r\n \t<li>The health care giant has struggled with staffing issues, like many in the industry, employing higher-cost temporary staffing during the pandemic\u2019s onset. A survey conducted by union researchers found that HCA staffed its hospitals at 30% below the national average in 2020. Responding to the survey, HCA said that its staffing was \u201csafe, appropriate,\" and \u201cin line\u201d with its industry.</li><br />\r\n \t<li>The company has repeatedly fought with the Federal Trade Commission over its acquisition of more hospitals, first in Utah in June 2022, and then in Louisiana in April 2023.</li><br />\r\n</ul>",
      "uri": "/ranking/fortune500/2023/",
      "__typename": "_ItemRankingListsType"
    },
