        "alt": "G500-2020-219-HCA-Healthcare",
        "caption": "Registered Nurses (RN) give pamphlets to a nurse arriving to work at the Medical Center of Trinity in Trinity, Florida, U.S., on Wednesday, April 1, 2020. Registered nurses at 15 HCA Healthcare hospitals in seven states held actions Wednesday to protest a lack of preparedness by the nation's largest hospital chain that they say places nurses, other staff, and patients at risk in the face of the coronavirus pandemic, announced National Nurses United (NNU) today. Photographer: Eve Edelheit/Bloomberg via Getty Images",
        "credit": "Eve EdelheitBloomberg/Getty Images",
        "__typename": "rankingListItemImage"
      },
      "itemDescription": "HCA Healthcare, a Nashville-based hospital operator, continued its steady growth in 2019 with annual revenue of $51.3 billion, an increase of nearly $5 billion from the previous year, even as net income dipped 5%. HCA's facility admissions have increased over the past year, and it now operates 184 hospitals, up from 179 the year before. Its hospitals treated 35 million patients in 2019, a new record. And the company's goal of expanding clinical capacity has been boosted by multimillion-dollar capital investments. Like many hospital operators, HCA is facing considerable financial pressure related to the coronavirus pandemic; this spring, it suspended its quarterly dividend.",
